<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revolution of Ayutla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1854–1855</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Revolution of Ayutla was a liberal uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> against the dictatorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a>, launched in March 1854 under the Plan of Ayutla, proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> with the backing of Juan Álvarez and Ignacio Comonfort. The revolt spread across the country, and Santa Anna left for exile in August 1855. Its victory brought liberals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> and Miguel Lerdo de Tejada to power and opened the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-reforma">La Reforma</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/revolution-of-ayutla.jpg" alt="Revolution of Ayutla" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SUN RISE (book), Oil (Unknown) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Alvarez.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
